<BaseLayout title="Star Wheel Conveyors: Starwheels & Timing Screws Explained" description="How a star wheel conveyor feeds bottles and cans into rotary fillers, cappers, and labelers, plus timing screw pitch, quick-change starwheels, and servo sync." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/star-wheel/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Star Wheel &amp; Timing Screw Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A star wheel conveyor is the transfer between a straight container conveyor and a rotary machine: a pocketed wheel (the rotary star wheel) picks round containers off the belt one at a time and sets each one into a filler, capper, or labeler station. A timing screw upstream spaces the containers so each arrives at an open pocket. Both are container-specific change parts, so changeover design matters as much as speed.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">What Is a Star Wheel Conveyor?</h2>
<p>A star wheel conveyor is an infeed or outfeed transfer that uses a rotating pocketed wheel, the starwheel, to move round containers one at a time between a straight conveyor and a rotary filler, capper, or labeler. A timing screw usually sits upstream to space containers so each one arrives at an open pocket.</p>
