<h3>How the Star Wheel Mechanism Works</h3>
<p>A star wheel is a rotating disc with evenly spaced semicircular pockets around its edge. Each pocket holds one container. The wheel turns in step with the rotary machine it feeds, so one pocket meets one machine station. As the wheel turns, each pocket picks a container off the infeed conveyor, carries it across a stationary dead plate while a curved center guide keeps it seated in the pocket, and sets it into the machine station (a filler valve, a capper chuck, a labeling station). On the outfeed side, a second star wheel takes each container out of the machine and puts it on the discharge conveyor.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/starwheel-septimatech-filler-star.webp" width="1000" height="752" alt="Two red plastic star wheels on a rotary machine, each pocket holding one clear wide-mouth jar, with a curved red guide holding the jars in the pockets." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Septimatech quick change star wheels and guide on a rotary filler and capper, with one jar seated in each pocket. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.septimatech.com/products-and-services/machine-changeovers/change-parts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Septimatech</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Star wheels are the bridge between linear conveyor flow and rotary machine motion. Containers arrive in single file on a flat conveyor. The <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">infeed</a> star wheel turns that linear stream into rotary motion, and the outfeed star wheel turns it back into linear flow.</p>
<p>Pocket geometry must match the container. A pocket that is too large lets the container shift and arrive at the station off-center. A pocket that is too small jams the container or damages its wall. That fit is why star wheels are container-specific change parts: a different container diameter or profile usually needs a different star wheel. Ask the supplier what pocket clearance they design for your container material and finish.</p>
<h2 id="screw-heading">Timing Screws &amp; Worm Screws</h2>
<h3>How Timing Screws Separate Containers</h3>
<p>A timing screw is a rotating helical screw mounted alongside the infeed conveyor, parallel to container travel. <a href="https://www.septimatech.com/blog/timing-screws-and-feed-screws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Septimatech</a>, a feed screw manufacturer, describes timing screws as controlling "the spacing, orientation, and timing of containers as they enter, exit, or transfer between packaging machinery." Containers arrive touching on the infeed belt. The helix engages each container in turn, and because the pitch (the distance between adjacent flights) increases from the infeed end to the discharge end, each container is gradually separated from the one behind it.</p>
<p>At the infeed end, the pitch is close to the container diameter, so touching containers fit between adjacent flights. As the container advances, the widening pitch opens a gap behind it. At the discharge end, the pitch matches the star wheel's pocket spacing. The container leaves the screw at the spacing and timing the star wheel needs to catch it.</p>
<figure class="video"><a class="video__link" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LGRy5HFRXSs" data-youtube="LGRy5HFRXSs"><img src="/img/photos/yt-lgry5hfrxss.webp" width="1000" height="563" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><span class="video__play">Play video: Feed Screw Drives / Timing Screw Drive Units - Increase Performance, Efficiency, &amp; Flexibility</span></a><figcaption>Septimatech feed screw drive units moving containers through single and dual timing screws. <span class="photo-credit">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@SeptimatechGroupInc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Septimatech Group Inc.</a> on YouTube</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>This progressive spacing is central to <a href="/conveyor-functions/metering/">metering</a> containers into rotary machines. Without the timing screw, containers reach the star wheel in an uncontrolled mass, which causes jams, double-feeds, and empty stations.</p>
<h3>Single vs. Dual Timing Screws</h3>
<p>Single-screw systems use one screw on one side of the conveyor with a guide rail on the other side. They handle stable round containers (PET bottles, cans, glass jars) and are the simpler, more common configuration.</p>
<p>Dual-screw systems use two screws, one on each side, turning in opposite directions. Both screws contact the container, which centers it and removes the side load a single screw applies. Septimatech states that dual screws "provide enhanced control and synchronization." They suit tall, narrow, or lightweight containers that tip easily, and higher-speed lines. Dual screws also improve <a href="/conveyor-functions/singulation/">singulation</a> because both sides of the container are positively controlled.</p>
<div class="photo-row"><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/starwheel-septimatech-infeed-screw.webp" width="1000" height="666" alt="A single dark green helical timing screw running alongside a row of large filled water bottles, with each bottle sitting in a flight of the screw." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Septimatech single infeed screw for soft-wall containers, with water bottles in the screw flights. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.septimatech.com/products-and-services/machine-changeovers/feed-screws-systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Septimatech</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/starwheel-septimatech-dual-screw.webp" width="1000" height="662" alt="Two red helical timing screws mounted on opposite sides of a tabletop chain conveyor, with the pitch of both screws widening along their length." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Septimatech retrofitted dual feed screws, one on each side of the conveyor chain. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.septimatech.com/products-and-services/machine-changeovers/feed-screws-systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Septimatech</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>
<h3>Pitch and Spacing Control</h3>
<p>The pitch profile is the core engineering parameter. The infeed pitch is set by container diameter, the discharge pitch by the star wheel pocket pitch, and the transition between them sets how hard each container is accelerated as it separates from the stream.</p>
<p>A linear pitch profile gives constant acceleration and is simple to cut, but abrupt engagement can tip containers at higher speed. Smoother, computed profiles reduce tipping and scuffing and are common on high-speed beverage lines, where the profile is designed around the container's center of gravity, friction, and line speed.</p>
<h2 id="changeover-heading">Change Parts &amp; Changeover</h2>
<p>Star wheels and timing screws are container-specific. Each container diameter, height, and shape needs a matched set of change parts: the timing screw, the infeed star wheel, the outfeed star wheel, the center guide, and guide rails. A line running five container sizes needs five sets. Timing screws are machined per container, so change parts are a meaningful line item in any multi-SKU project, and storage and handling of those parts is part of the design.</p>
<p>For lines in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/beverage/">beverage</a> and <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">pharmaceutical</a> environments, store change parts in labeled racks or carts to prevent damage, contamination, and mix-ups. A dropped screw or one stored loose against other metal parts can get nicks in the flight edges that cause scuffing, jams, and uneven spacing.</p>
<h2 id="quick-change">Quick-Change Star Wheel Systems</h2>
<p>A quick-change star wheel system replaces bolted change parts with tool-less mounts (hand knobs, cam locks, locating pins, or sliding carriages) so each part drops into a fixed, repeatable position without adjustment. The goal is a changeover that one operator can do from a written procedure, without a maintenance technician.</p>
<p>Published figures show the size of the gap. <a href="https://www.nerconconveyors.com/Nercon-Blog/Star-Wheel-Innovation-Results-in-Faster-Product-Changeover" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nercon</a> describes a conventional bolted fixture swap that "can take a maintenance person 30 minutes to an hour," and a redesigned star wheel on a sliding carriage with a pin lock that slides into place "within seconds." The <a href="https://making.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Krones_6102_brochure.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Krones Modulfill Bloc FS-C brochure</a> lists quick-change times with one operator of 1.5 minutes for the infeed worm and 3.1 minutes for the infeed starwheel, and compares quick-change guidance parts (2.0 minutes) with 18 minutes for standard guidance parts on that filler. Treat these as single-machine examples, not general benchmarks.</p>
<h3>Adding Quick-Change Parts to Existing Conveyor Equipment</h3>
<p>Quick-change star wheel systems can often be retrofitted to existing conveyors and machines. Before ordering, confirm:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mounting:</strong> the existing shaft, hub, and drive interface, and whether the new hub keeps the original timing position.</li>
<li><strong>Machine geometry:</strong> pitch circle diameter, pocket count, and the handoff point to the machine. The new parts must match the machine, not just the container.</li>
<li><strong>Full set:</strong> whether the timing screw, center guide, and guide rails also get quick-change mounts. A tool-less star wheel saves little if the guides still need wrenches.</li>
<li><strong>Repeatability:</strong> how the mount locates the part (pins, keyed hubs, color coding) so no fine adjustment is needed after the swap.</li>
<li><strong>Guarding:</strong> whether operators can reach the mounts without defeating guards. OSHA's <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">29 CFR 1910.212</a> requires guarding of rotating parts and nip points.</li>
<li><strong>Proof:</strong> a measured changeover time on a comparable retrofit, and who supplies future parts for new containers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Servo-driven timing screws reduce, but do not remove, change parts. Changing the servo speed profile adjusts spacing for nearby container sizes, but the screw's physical helix still fits a limited diameter range, so larger size changes still need a different screw. Ask the supplier which of your containers one screw can cover.</p>
<h2 id="sync-heading">Timing Screw (Infeed Worm) Synchronization</h2>
<p>A timing screw, which Krones calls the infeed worm, is a rotating rod with a spiral groove cut along its length. Each turn of the screw moves every container forward by one flight. Synchronization means the screw delivers exactly one container to each star wheel pocket, at the moment that pocket reaches the handoff point. Two things must match: the screw's discharge pitch must equal the star wheel pocket pitch, and the screw's rotation must stay locked in phase with the star wheel and the machine.</p>
<p>The timing screw and star wheel must turn in exact step with the rotary machine. If a container arrives early, the pocket is not in position and the container hits the solid section between pockets. If it arrives late, the pocket passes empty and the machine station runs without a container. As a worked example, a machine running 1,200 containers per minute receives one container every 50 milliseconds, so small timing errors matter.</p>
<h3>Mechanical Synchronization</h3>
<p>Traditional systems use a gear train or timing belt connecting the timing screw shaft, star wheel shaft, and machine main shaft. All three turn at fixed ratios: the screw makes one revolution per N containers (N = number of helix starts), and the star wheel makes one revolution per M containers (M = number of pockets). The physical connection prevents timing drift. The drawback is inflexibility: ratio changes need gear changes, and the whole system starts and stops together.</p>
<h3>Electronic (Servo) Synchronization</h3>
<p>Modern systems put servo motors on the timing screw and star wheel and lock them to the machine's master encoder through electronic cam profiles in the PLC or motion controller. This allows speed changes on the fly, soft-start ramps that keep containers from tipping, position offsets for fine-tuning placement, controlled stops during machine faults, and recipe-based changeover where the controller loads a different cam profile for each container.</p>
<h3>What Happens When the Screw Is Out of Phase</h3>
<p>Phase is the angular position of the screw relative to the star wheel. Correct speed with wrong phase still fails, because every container arrives at the same wrong point of the pocket. Typical symptoms:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Container hits the pocket tip.</strong> The container arrives early or late and strikes the point between pockets. Result: tipped containers, jams, chipped glass, or dented cans.</li>
<li><strong>Empty pockets and stations.</strong> A container misses its pocket and the machine station runs empty, or a container is squeezed into the next pocket.</li>
<li><strong>Scuffing and noise at the handoff.</strong> A small phase error shows up as rubbing between the container, the screw flight, and the pocket before it shows up as jams.</li>
<li><strong>Drift after a stop.</strong> On servo systems, a lost reference after a fault or power loss can restart the screw out of phase. On mechanical drives, a slipped belt or worn coupling does the same.</li>
</ul>
<p>Mechanical systems are phased once by setting the screw's angular position on its shaft or coupling during setup. Servo systems store the phase offset in the motion controller and re-home to the machine encoder on start. After any change part swap, check the handoff by jogging the machine slowly with containers before running at speed, and confirm the controller loaded the right recipe for the container.</p>
<h2 id="container-heading">Container Handling</h2>
<p>Star wheels and timing screws are built mainly for round containers. Square, rectangular, or irregular containers need shaped screws and pockets or a different infeed, such as servo-driven lugs, bucket conveyors, or indexing belts. See <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">infeed and outfeed systems</a> for the alternatives.</p>
<p>Container material affects screw design. Glass is heavy and rigid and tolerates contact pressure but can chip from impact. PET bottles are light and flexible and deform under excess screw pressure, which causes uneven spacing. Thin-walled aluminum cans dent easily, so screws and pockets that contact them are usually made from engineering plastics such as UHMW polyethylene or acetal rather than metal.</p>
<p>Container stability is the main limit on line speed. Tall, narrow containers with high centers of gravity tip at lower speeds than short, wide ones. The screw profile, guide rail height, and belt friction must all support the container at the target speed. Empty lightweight PET bottles are the hardest: they blow over from air currents, tip from vibration, and float on wet conveyors.</p>
<h2 id="applications-heading">Industry Applications</h2>
<h3>Beverage Filling Lines</h3>
<p>Beverage lines are the largest application. High-speed rotary fillers set the pace: Krones rates its Modulfill Bloc FS-C at up to 135,000 cans per hour (about 2,250 per minute). At these rates, screw and star wheel precision and fast changeover drive line efficiency. Multi-start timing screws (two or three helical flights on one screw) increase throughput without raising screw speed.</p>
<h3>Pharmaceutical Bottle Lines</h3>
<p>Pharmaceutical bottle lines usually run slower than beverage lines but demand cleanability and documented control. Change parts are typically stainless steel or approved plastics with sanitary finishes, and tool-less disassembly supports cleaning validation. Where serialization applies, the transfer must keep containers in sequence so each one stays matched to its record.</p>
<h3>Capping and Labeling Machine Infeed</h3>
<p>Rotary cappers and labelers use star wheels the same way fillers do. Capping star wheels must place the container directly under the cap delivery point. Small misalignment causes cross-threaded or missed caps. Labeling transfers must present the container consistently for label registration, sometimes with an orientation device at the transfer.</p>
<SpecChecklist id="spec-checklist" title="Star wheel and change parts specification checklist" items={[
