<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a star wheel on a packaging line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A star wheel (starwheel or rotary star wheel) is a rotating wheel with evenly spaced pockets that takes round containers one at a time from a conveyor and places them into the stations of a rotary filler, capper, or labeler. A second star wheel takes containers back out onto the outfeed conveyor. The wheel turns in sync with the machine, one pocket per station, and a timing screw upstream spaces containers so each arrives at an open pocket.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a timing screw on a bottle line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A timing screw (worm screw or feed screw) is a helical screw that separates touching containers into evenly spaced units. Its increasing pitch gradually spaces containers apart as they travel along it. At the discharge end, spacing matches the star wheel pocket pitch for a synchronized handoff to the rotary machine.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What are change parts for star wheels and timing screws?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Change parts are container-specific components swapped when switching container sizes: the timing screw, star wheels, center guide, and guide rails. Changeover time depends on the mounting design. Nercon describes a bolted fixture swap taking 30 minutes to an hour. Krones lists 1.5 minutes for the infeed worm and 3.1 minutes for the infeed starwheel on its Modulfill Bloc FS-C, with one operator. Each new container size needs its own set.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do you synchronize a timing screw with a rotary filler?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Synchronization uses mechanical gearing (gears or timing belts connecting screw, star wheel, and machine shafts at fixed ratios) or servo coupling (electronic cam profiles in the PLC or motion controller following the machine's position). Servo systems allow speed changes and soft-start ramps. Either way, the screw's exit pitch must match the star wheel pocket pitch.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Can you add quick-change star wheels to existing conveyor equipment?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Often, yes. Retrofits replace bolted star wheel and screw mounts with tool-less hubs, locating pins, or sliding carriages so parts drop into a fixed, repeatable position. Before ordering, confirm the shaft and hub design, the machine's pitch circle and pocket count, guard access, and whether the guide rails and center guide also need quick-change mounts. Ask for the measured changeover time on a comparable retrofit.</p>

</div>
